-- Verified audit design from Supabase inspection:
--   * audit_log.id is GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY.
--   * audit_log.audit_id is GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY and is the primary key.
--   * Each column uses its own associated identity sequence.
--   * Omit BOTH columns from every audit INSERT. Do not expect their values to
--     match, and do not generate or provide either value in application code.
--   * user_permission currently has no audit trigger. Choose exactly one
--     mechanism: explicit INSERT in the transaction, or a reviewed trigger.
--     This design chooses explicit INSERT and must not be combined with a
--     permission audit trigger.
--
-- Schema drift to resolve before turning this into a migration:
--   * Live app_user / user_permission identifiers are UUID; repository
--     migration 003 defines BIGINT IDs and a composite key.
--   * Live user_permission has no unique constraint; migration 003 does.
--   * The supplied DB Admin note says its migration 008 defines only id as
--     GENERATED ALWAYS. In this repository, db/migrations/008_protect_last_superadmin.sql
--     does not define audit_log. Identify the exact migration/version before
--     attempting reconciliation.
--   * Verify all audit_log required columns, their types/defaults, existing
--     audit triggers, permission actor-guard triggers, and service_role grants.
--
-- Proposed RPC contract (not a claim that this RPC exists):
--   public.manage_user_permissions(p_actor_id UUID, p_target_user_id UUID,
--                                  p_right_ids BIGINT[]) RETURNS INTEGER
-- No definition is present in the tracked repository. Live database existence
-- and definition are unverified; do not rely on an RPC until the DB Admin
-- confirms it. Any new/changed RPC body and policy predicates must be reviewed
-- against the live schema before implementation:
--
-- 1. The authenticated server resolves the actor from Supabase Auth and sends
--    only that verified actor ID, target ID, and the complete selected rights
--    set to a server-only RPC. Never accept actor identity from the browser.
-- 2. In the RPC transaction, verify actor active/approved/unblocked status;
--    lock the target app_user row FOR UPDATE to serialize permission changes;
--    reject protected targets and enforce the approved actor/target/right
--    policy. Until approval, reject all writes.
-- 3. Check submitted right IDs against the active catalogue. Detect existing
--    duplicate Active (user_id, right_id) rows and fail closed for reviewed
--    cleanup; do not silently delete or collapse history. To guarantee
--    concurrency-safe uniqueness, have the DB Admin review a partial unique
--    index on (user_id, right_id) WHERE status='Active' after duplicate rows
--    have been resolved by audited status updates. Do not rely on application
--    checks or target-row locks alone.
-- 4. For each newly selected grant, reactivate an existing Inactive row or
--    insert one if none exists. The target-row lock prevents concurrent RPCs
--    from creating duplicate active rows, and the reviewed partial unique
--    index provides a database-level backstop.
-- 5. For each unchecked active assignment allowed by the approved revoke
--    policy, UPDATE status to 'Inactive' (never DELETE). Capture old/new row
--    values so each actual state change has one corresponding audit record.
-- 6. For EACH actual grant/revoke, insert exactly one audit row in the same
--    transaction. Set table_name='user_permission', action='GRANTED' or
--    'REVOKED', record_key to identify target and right, old_values/new_values
--    to include target user ID and right ID, action_by=public.audit_user(), and
--    timestamps from CURRENT_TIMESTAMP. Omit id and audit_id from the column
--    list. Confirm any additional required audit columns with the DB Admin.
-- 7. Any permission or audit error must abort the whole RPC transaction. Once
--    approved, revoke direct application-role INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on
--    user_permission so callers cannot bypass the RPC; preserve needed reads.
--
